// The orders sheet has order amounts in column D.
// D2 = 4500 (LG-2001), D3 = 7200 (LG-2002)

// Task 1: In E1, write a formula that computes D3 minus D2.
// Expected result: 2700

// Task 2: In E2, write a formula that adds D2 and D3, then multiplies by 2.
// Use parentheses so the addition happens before the multiplication.
// Expected result: 23400

// H1 holds the shared tax rate.
H1: 0.075

// Task: In E2, write a formula that multiplies D2 by the tax rate in H1.
// Use $H$1 (absolute) for the tax rate so it does not shift when copied.
// Use D2 (relative) for the amount so it adjusts to D3, D4 when copied down.
// Then write the same pattern in E3 and E4.

// The orders sheet has delivery_minutes in column C.
// Input cells (typed constants - the gate will mutate these):
orders!C2: 28
orders!C3: 25
orders!C4: 56
orders!C5: 19

// Task 1: In F2, write a nested IF formula that classifies C2 as:
//   'Late'    if C2 > 60
//   'On Time' if C2 >= 30 AND C2 <= 60  (use AND for both bounds)
//   'Fast'    if C2 < 30
// Then write the same pattern in F3 and F4.

// Task 2: In F5, write an IF formula that returns 'Extreme' if
//   C5 < 20 OR C5 > 70, and 'Normal' otherwise. Use OR.

// H1 holds the reference date as a serial number (45361 = 2024-03-10).
H1: 45361
// G2 and G3 hold order-date serial numbers (45355 = 2024-03-04).
G2: 45355
G3: 45355

// Task 1: In I2, write a DATEDIF formula that returns the number of days
// between the order date in G2 and the reference date in H1.
// DATEDIF syntax: DATEDIF(start_date, end_date, unit)
// Use unit "D" for days. Expected result: 6

// Task 2: In I3, write the same DATEDIF formula for G3.
// Expected result: 6

// The drivers sheet has driver_id in column A and driver_name in column B.
// A1 holds the driver ID you want to look up.
A1: D01

// Task 1: In B1, write a VLOOKUP formula that looks up the driver ID in A1
// in the drivers sheet and returns the driver name (column 2). Use FALSE for exact match.

// Task 2: In C1, write an XLOOKUP formula that looks up the driver ID in A1
// in drivers column A and returns the matching driver name from drivers column B.

// The drivers sheet has driver names in column A and driver IDs in column B.
// C1 holds the driver ID you want to look up.
C1: D01

// Task: In cell D1, write an INDEX/MATCH formula that looks up the driver ID in C1
// in column B and returns the corresponding driver name from column A.
// Use match_type 0 for an exact match.
// Since you're working in the same sheet as the data, use A:A and B:B (not drivers!A:A).

// This workbook has three broken formulas. Fix all three.
//
// Row 8: A8 holds a driver ID to look up. B8 uses VLOOKUP to find the driver name.
// The lookup is returning #N/A because the driver ID in A8 is misspelled.
// Fix A8 so the driver ID matches exactly what is in the drivers sheet (use D01).
// The drivers data is in rows 1-6 (A1:B6).
A8: D0X
B8: =VLOOKUP(A8,A1:B6,2,0)

// C8 has a #REF! error. It should sum E1 and F1.
// Replace the formula in C8 with =SUM(E1:F1).
C8: =#REF!
E1: 100
F1: 200

// Row 9: D9 adds cells A9, B9, and C9. It returns #VALUE! because B9 contains text.
// Fix D9 by replacing the arithmetic addition with a function that ignores text values.
A9: 100
B9: N/A
C9: 200
D9: =A9+B9+C9

// The '2024Q1' sheet is mounted and contains order data.
// Header is in row 1; data starts from row 2.
// Column B in '2024Q1' holds the city for each order.
//
// Task: In cell Z1 of this sheet, write a formula that pulls the value
// from cell B4 on the sheet named '2024Q1'.
// Note: the sheet name starts with a number, so it must be enclosed in single quotes.
